[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m So the observable behaviour is: **print the last integer successfully read, or
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0` if none were read; always exit 0; never write to stderr.** `out[0] = 0`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m before the `fma_array` call is dead for every `len >= 1`, since `fma_array`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m overwrites all of `out`. The uninitialised `int data[100]` is never read beyond
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m index `i-1`, so it has no observable effect: when `i == 0` the `len == 0` early
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m return means `data` is not touched at all.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Divergence risks audited
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 1. `scanf` skips whitespace across newlines (not line-oriented)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C: one `scanf("%d")` consumes arbitrary leading whitespace, newlines included,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so line structure is irrelevant. A translation written with `read_line` +
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `trim().parse()` would break on `"1\n\n\n2"`, on `"1 2 3"` on one line, and on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m whitespace-only input.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `scanf_d` loops `getc` while `is_space`, matching glibc's `isspace` set
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m exactly — space, `\t`, `\n`, `\x0b` (VT), `\x0c` (FF), `\r`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified: `scanf_reads_across_newlines`, `multiple_items_various_separators`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `whitespace_only_input`. Mutations that narrowed the whitespace set were caught:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m dropping VT/FF failed 2 tests; skipping only `' '` failed 5.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 2. Integer conversion truncates, it does not saturate
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C: glibc's `%d` converts with `strtol` semantics into a `long` (saturating at
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `LONG_MAX`/`LONG_MIN` on overflow, with `ERANGE`) and then **assigns that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `long` to an `int`**, truncating to the low 32 bits. The consequences are
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m counter-intuitive and were confirmed against the real binary:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input | C prints | why |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `2147483648` (`INT_MAX+1`) | `-2147483648` | fits a `long`, truncated to `int` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4294967296` (`2^32`) | `0` | low 32 bits are zero |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775807` (`LONG_MAX`) | `-1` | low 32 bits are all ones |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `9223372036854775808` (`LONG_MAX+1`) | `-1` | saturates to `LONG_MAX`, then truncates |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-9223372036854775809` | `0` | saturates to `LONG_MIN`, then truncates |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 400 `9`s | `-1` | saturates to `LONG_MAX`, then truncates |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `-` + 400 `9`s | `0` | saturates to `LONG_MIN`, then truncates |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m A `str::parse::<i32>()`-based translation would reject all of these, and an
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i32`-clamping translation would print `2147483647`/`-2147483648` instead.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: accumulates the magnitude in a `u64`, tracks overflow against the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strtol` cutoff (`i64::MAX`, or `i64::MAX + 1` when negative), saturates to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i64::MAX`/`i64::MIN`, then does `as i32` for the truncating assignment.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified: `int_boundaries`, `long_boundaries_and_saturation`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `very_long_digit_strings`, `overflow_values_in_the_middle_and_at_the_end`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Mutations were caught: removing positive saturation failed 4 tests; clamping to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `i32` range instead of truncating failed 5.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 3. Leading zeros are decimal, never octal
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C: `%d` is base 10 unconditionally (unlike `%i`), so `010` is ten and 500 zeros
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m followed by `7` is seven.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: plain base-10 digit accumulation, no `0`/`0x` prefix handling.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
