[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 4. Sign handling, including signs that lead nowhere
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C: `%d` accepts one optional `+` or `-`. A sign not followed by a digit is a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failure, and glibc can only push back one character, so the sign is consumed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and lost. `--5`, `+-5`, `- 5`, `-\n5` and a lone `-` at EOF all fail the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m conversion, which breaks the loop.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: consumes at most one sign; if the next byte is not a digit it `ungetc`s
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that byte and returns 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Verified: `sign_handling_and_lone_signs`. Removing `+` support failed 2 tests.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
