[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m failure, but `main` only tests `!= 1`, so the two are indistinguishable here.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m The `ungetc` is likewise unobservable, because the failure immediately `break`s
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the loop and nothing reads the stream again. Mutants that changed only these
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m details survived, correctly — they are behaviourally equivalent, not gaps.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ### 5. The 100-item cap and the unread tail
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C: `for (i = 0; i < 100; i++)` stops after 100 successful reads. Item 101 and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m anything after it stay in the stream unread, so a malformed or overflowing tail
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m after 100 valid items has no effect on the output.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust: `while i < 100` with the same break condition.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
